Indeed contracts enable this. Let's take an imaginary database contract:

(in pseudo code)

```nix
{
  options = {
    output = {
      endpoint = mkOption { type = str; };
    };
  };
}
```

Usually one would ask the Postgres database provider module to create a database for a consumer:

```nix
{
  postgres.databases.mydatabase = {};
  consumer.database = postgres.databases.mydatabase;
}
```

But nothing stops us from hardcoding it:

```nix
{
  consumer.database = {
    output.endpoint = "postgres://node2/mydatabase";
  };
}
```

This takes care of enabling any module using contracts to be node agnostic. But we still have the issue of how to actually coordinate values across nodes.
